      "title": "Can a social reflex stop a robot but never restart it? Testing a dual-dwell safety gate (Webots)",
      "link": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/can-a-social-reflex-stop-a-robot-but-never-restart-it-testing-a-dual-dwell-safety-gate-webots/59167",
      "summary": "Hi everyone, Following up on my earlier experiments with supervisory gates (Demo 8) : Webots Demo 8: Supervisory Gate Multi-Fault Lifecycle & Watchdog Hardening (QERRA-v2 Classical) I’ve built a new test to evaluate the recovery side of a safety event. My underlying vision in robotics is harmonious human-robot social collaboration —specifically in vulnerable spaces like hospitals or eldercare facilities & beyond. We want robots to react when someone calls for help or collapses. But here is the friction I constantly see: to make a robot socially aware, we have to rely on probabilistic AI models. If we let an uncertified perception model dictate the motor controllers, a lagging thread or hallucination can turn a 70 kg robot into a runaway hazard. I wanted to build a deterministic “floor” underneath the AI. The safety contract I wanted to test is simple: Perception can take away the permission to move, but it can never give it back. Before sharing the data, I want to be completely upfront. I am not a classically trained robotics engineer or a certified safety assessor. I am an independent solo developer in Greece, and I build this with AI coding tools (Claude, Gemini). I am sharing th",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-25T15:59:09+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-25T15:59:09+00:00",
      "source": "ROS2 Discourse",
      "section_hint": "로보틱스",
      "articleText": "Can a social reflex stop a robot but never restart it? Testing a dual-dwell safety gate (Webots) - Projects - Open Robotics Discourse Can a social reflex stop a robot but never restart it? Testing a dual-dwell safety gate (Webots) Projects webots , safety marunigno-ship-it October 25, 2026, 3:59pm 1 Hi everyone, Following up on my earlier experiments with supervisory gates (Demo 8) : Webots Demo 8: Supervisory Gate Multi-Fault Lifecycle & Watchdog Hardening (QERRA-v2 Classical) I’ve built a new test to evaluate the recovery side of a safety event. My underlying vision in robotics is harmonious human-robot social collaboration —specifically in vulnerable spaces like hospitals or eldercare facilities & beyond. We want robots to react when someone calls for help or collapses. But here is the friction I constantly see: to make a robot socially aware, we have to rely on probabilistic AI models. If we let an uncertified perception model dictate the motor controllers, a lagging thread or hallucination can turn a 70 kg robot into a runaway hazard. I wanted to build a deterministic “floor” underneath the AI. The safety contract I wanted to test is simple: Perception can take away the permission to move, but it can never give it back. Before sharing the data, I want to be completely upfront. I am not a classically trained robotics engineer or a certified safety assessor. I am an independent solo developer in Greece, and I build this with AI coding tools (Claude, Gemini). I am sharing this here because I want to learn from those of you actually deploying robots around humans, and see if this architecture holds up. (If i ever get an answer! ). The Architecture: Doer-Checker I built a dual-channel pattern and tested it on a TIAGo base in Webots: The “Doer” (Navigation Brain) : An unprivileged process that proposes velocities (2.00 rad/s) over UDP. It has no direct access to the wheels. The “Checker” (Supervisory Gate) : A pure mathematical function step(state, params, inputs) → StepResult running on a strict 32 ms cycle. It is the only thing that writes to the actuators. The Reflex Layer (QERRA-HSR) : Evaluates distress. It enforces a 1.0s hysteresis dwell before it steps down from CRITICAL to CLEAR, preventing the robot from “chattering” if camera confidence fluctuates. The Latch : After any trip, the Gate requires the cause to be clear for a continuous 1.0s, followed by an explicit operator reset. The Run (14.0 Seconds, 438 Control Cycles) This is a single continuous simulation take. Times below are from the audit log. Sim time What happens Gate Response 0.03 s Boot TRIPPED (proposer not heard yet) 1.312 s Scripted reset Accepted → RUN at 2.00 rad/s 4.000 s Distress injected (0.85 conf), HSR CRITICAL TRIPPED on the same 32ms cycle (cycle 125), wheels clamped to 0.000 5.024 s Scripted reset while distress is active Refused (RESET_REJECTED_HAZARD) 7.008 s Injected distress removed Still TRIPPED 8.032 s HSR back to CLEAR (after 1.0s hysteresis dwell) Still TRIPPED, resets refused (RESET_REJECTED_NOT_READY) 9.056 s Reset on the first allowed cycle Accepted → RUN at 2.00 rad/s 14.016 s End of run, log closed Note: “Same cycle” means the wheel command went to 0.000 on the exact control cycle the status became CRITICAL. (Time spent computing the Gate decision in Python: p50 = 1.38 ms, max = 4.50 ms). A quick lesson learned: I initially predicted recovery at 8.2s. But there are actually two dwells stacking here: the reflex layer’s 1.0s dwell (to ensure the scene is calm), followed by the Gate’s own 1.0s internal safety dwell. The earliest reset possible was 9.03s. It was a great lesson in why writing down expected cycles before running is so important. Replay Audit Because the Gate is a pure function, every cycle is hash-chained into a JSONL log. Using an offline verifier (verify_log.py --replay), I fed the recorded inputs back through the function. All 438 cycles reproduced bit-for-bit with 0 execution divergences . (Head hash: eccb0d85416cf4a651205f34f3cc0d9275bff6e2b3baa512e4b23dbef0c2714a). (Transparency note: While the replay is deterministic, live UDP on Windows is not. An earlier take of this scenario tripped at 10.2s due to a host scheduling load that stalled the proposer for 160ms. The gate failed-closed exactly as designed). Honest Boundaries (What this does not show) No live detection. The 0.85 distress flag is a constant injected by my test script. Real CV detection (with false positives/negatives) is the hardest part, and is not tested here. No human in the loop. The resets are injected by a script to test the exact microsecond boundaries. Because of this, the video shows the robot auto-restarting after the dwells finish. In reality, a human must assess the scene before hitting that reset button. Not a certification claim. This is an independent simulation (Webots/Windows/Python), not an industrial RTOS. I am not affiliated with PAL Robotics. My Questions for the Community If you run robots in shared spaces (hospitals, retail): after a protective stop caused by a person, what is your actual restart rule? Remote confirmation? A physical button on the chassis? Is “perception may only stop the robot, but never start it” a contract you use in practice, or do you let perception modify trajectories directly inside the safety layer? In a real ROS 2 stack, I envision putting a node like this directly between the final cmd_vel mux and the base controller, blocking all other publishers. Has anyone implemented a strict latch-and-reset pattern this deep in the stack? What went wrong? Links: Video (31s, Webots window, unedited): Repository (Apache-2.0): GitHub - marunigno-ship-it/QERRA-v2-classical: Three-layer deterministic safety & ethical execution guard for autonomous robots (ROS 2 & Behavior Trees). Sub-ms physical reflex (QERRA-HSR) + moral filter (SEMEV-12) + values ranker (THRIVE). Fully auditable. · GitHub Evidence logs: QERRA-v2-classical/docs/demo9 at main · marunigno-ship-it/QERRA-v2-classical · GitHub Thank you for your time, and I welcome any brutal feedback you have on this architecture. Marussa 1 Like Related topics Topic Replies Views Activity Webots Demo 7: Testing a Supervisory Gate Pattern on TIAGo (QERRA-v2 Classical) Projects ros2 , webots , open-source 1 92 October 16, 2026 Webots Demo 6: Compound Physical Safety Reflex on TIAGo (QERRA-v2 Classical) Projects ros2 , webots , safetylayer 0 52 September 24, 2026 QERRA-v2 Demo 5: Closed-Loop Moral Gating, Physical Safety, and Values Selection (TIAGo in Webots) Projects ros2 , webots , safety , hri , behaviortrees 5 163 September 13, 2026 Webots Demo 8: Supervisory Gate Multi-Fault Lifecycle & Watchdog Hardening (QERRA-v2 Classical) Projects ros2 , webots , safety , opensource 0 32 October 21, 2026 QERRA-v2 Demo 3: Moral Execution Guard for Humanoid AMRs (PAL Robotics TIAGo in Webots) Projects ros2 , demo , webots , behaviortrees , cobots 0 73 August 4, 2026",
